 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 3,
   "plaque": "sthelena_up",
   "title": "Up the Hill",
   "text": "From Jamestown the road climbs the cliff in loops to the cool, green interior. It is cooler up here by several degrees. The island's highest point, Diana's Peak, is 823 metres, and from it you can see most of St Helena, and a very great deal of sea."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "sthelena_plantation",
   "title": "Plantation House",
   "text": "The Governor's official residence since the 1790s, a Georgian house with a long lawn. It is also home to Jonathan, a Seychelles giant tortoise who arrived in 1882, already fully grown. He is thought to be the oldest living land animal on Earth, which has been his only career."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "sthelena_longwood",
   "title": "Longwood House",
   "text": "Napoleon Bonaparte was exiled to St Helena in 1815 after Waterloo, and lived at Longwood House until he died there on 5 May 1821. The house and its grounds, and his tomb, have been French property since 1858, and a French honorary consul looks after them. The French flag flies above them."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 44,
   "plaque": "sthelena_tomb",
   "title": "Napoleon's Tomb",
   "text": "In a quiet valley under willow trees, Napoleon was buried in 1821, in a grave with no name, because the British and French could not agree on how to inscribe it. His remains were taken to Paris in 1840 and now lie in Les Invalides. The empty tomb remains, and so do the willows."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "sthelena_wirebird",
   "title": "The Wirebird",
   "text": "The St Helena plover, called the wirebird because of its thin legs, lives nowhere else in the world. It is the island's national bird. It nests on open pasture and so is easily disturbed by dogs, cats and people. The conservation team asks everyone to keep to the paths."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 44,
   "plaque": "sthelena_dianas",
   "title": "Diana's Peak",
   "text": "The island's highest point, wrapped in a thin remnant of the tree-fern forest that once covered the interior. The tree ferns and the other endemic plants are kept alive by the conservation team's work, with goats and invasive plants cleared to give them a chance."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 34,
   "plaque": "sthelena_deadwood",
   "title": "Deadwood Plain",
   "text": "From 1900 to 1902 nearly six thousand Boer prisoners of war were held on the island, in camps here on Deadwood Plain and at Broadbottom. The grass here had been a Napoleon-era military camp too. Today it is open farmland and the best place on the island to spot a wirebird."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 32,
   "plaque": "sthelena_flax",
   "title": "The Flax Years",
   "text": "Between about 1907 and 1965 the island's main industry was New Zealand flax, grown for rope and fibre and processed in mills. When plastic rope arrived, the market collapsed and the island lost its main income. The remaining flax now grows wild on the hills."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "sthelena_gumwood",
   "title": "The Gumwood Forest",
   "text": "Before goats, felling and ships, St Helena was covered in gumwood and ebony forest, found nowhere else. By the 1700s most had gone, and several kinds of plant were lost for ever. The St Helena olive was lost for good in 2003. A small number of gumwoods are now being planted again."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 49,
   "text": "NAPOLEON'S TOMB. The empty grave of the Emperor, under willow trees. His body was taken to Paris in 1840. The willows have stayed."
  }
 ],
